**My Timetable** is your own week, one tab per day. It is read-only by design — the timetable is
built centrally so that one person's change cannot double-book someone else. See
[Generate the Timetable](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-generate-timetable) for how it is produced.

## Reading it

Each day tab lists your periods in order with the class, the subject and the room. Free periods and
breaks are part of the day, drawn from the bell schedule your institute uses.

## The SUB marker

A period flagged **SUB** is one you are covering for an absent colleague. It is not part of your
normal timetable and it will not be there tomorrow — it comes from the day's substitution plan.

Two things follow from that:

- **Check My Timetable in the morning, not just at the start of term.** A substitution added today
  appears today.
- A SUB period is somebody else's class. You are covering it, not taking it over, and it disappears
  from your week when the plan for that day does.

If you think a SUB assignment is wrong, it is changed in the substitution plan by whoever built it —
see [Substitution Duties](/docs/educonnect-teacher-substitution).

## If your timetable is empty

Either the timetable has not been generated yet for this term, or the subjects you teach are not
assigned to you against any class. The second is the more common one, and it is fixed in
**Timetable → Class Subjects** by an administrator — see
[Assign Subjects and Teachers to a Class](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-class-subjects).

